WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:02.402
A difficult conversation.

2
00:00:03.149 --> 00:00:05.379
According to Stone, Patton and Heen,

3
00:00:05.611 --> 00:00:09.791
in their book Difficult Conversations from 1999,

4
00:00:10.011 --> 00:00:14.040
every difficult conversation is really three conversations at once.

5
00:00:15.043 --> 00:00:18.654
The first is about what happened: the facts, and who is right.

6
00:00:19.715 --> 00:00:23.894
The second is the feelings conversation: anger, embarrassment, hurt.

7
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.702
The third is the identity conversation: what does this say about me?

8
00:00:29.411 --> 00:00:30.293
Am I competent?

9
00:00:31.058 --> 00:00:32.486
Am I taken seriously?

10
00:00:33.488 --> 00:00:36.704
Under pressure the identity conversation runs loudest,

11
00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:39.397
especially when you are the youngest in the room.

12
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:43.599
You notice it in yourself as the urge to prove you are right.

13
00:00:44.955 --> 00:00:47.231
So prepare the conversation on paper.

14
00:00:47.936 --> 00:00:50.096
Fifteen minutes is usually enough.

15
00:00:51.065 --> 00:00:51.936
What did you see?

16
00:00:52.615 --> 00:00:54.124
What is probably their story?

17
00:00:54.818 --> 00:00:56.385
And what do you feel yourself?

18
00:00:57.392 --> 00:00:58.704
What was your contribution?

19
00:00:59.438 --> 00:01:02.608
What did you do, or leave undone, that helped create this?

20
00:01:03.626 --> 00:01:05.751
The book swaps blame for contribution.

21
00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:10.141
Because blame looks back, and contribution helps you fix it.

22
00:01:11.199 --> 00:01:12.441
What do you want at the end?

23
00:01:13.204 --> 00:01:14.644
One concrete agreement.

24
00:01:15.469 --> 00:01:19.173
Then write the first two sentences, and say them out loud.

25
00:01:19.962 --> 00:01:22.830
The opening is where most conversations go wrong.

26
00:01:24.154 --> 00:01:26.372
Open straight, with the third story:

27
00:01:26.523 --> 00:01:29.878
how a neutral observer would describe the difference between you.

28
00:01:31.052 --> 00:01:34.361
For example: I would like to talk about the downtime data.

29
00:01:35.118 --> 00:01:39.043
We see it differently, and I want to understand how you look at it.

30
00:01:40.202 --> 00:01:41.328
No long warm-up.

31
00:01:42.064 --> 00:01:44.340
People feel it coming, and tense up.

32
00:01:45.428 --> 00:01:47.425
Opinions differ on small talk.

33
00:01:48.162 --> 00:01:50.925
In a Dutch workplace one sentence is plenty.

34
00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:54.056
In some cultures skipping it feels rude.

35
00:01:55.097 --> 00:01:56.897
And never open by e-mail.

36
00:01:57.571 --> 00:02:00.764
Tone gets lost, and positions harden in writing.

37
00:02:02.064 --> 00:02:05.187
In the conversation, stay with facts and feelings.

38
00:02:05.903 --> 00:02:07.993
Separate what happened from what you made of it.

39
00:02:09.088 --> 00:02:10.830
Name emotions when they show.

40
00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:13.682
For example: you sound frustrated.

41
00:02:14.809 --> 00:02:16.713
Then intent versus impact.

42
00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.724
You know your impact.

43
00:02:19.464 --> 00:02:21.159
Only they know their intent.

44
00:02:22.202 --> 00:02:23.758
So don't accuse; ask.

45
00:02:24.488 --> 00:02:26.345
I don't think you meant it this way,

46
00:02:26.473 --> 00:02:29.259
but it made it harder for me to get people to talk.

47
00:02:30.001 --> 00:02:31.162
What did you mean by it?

48
00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.063
End with an agreement: who does what by when, and how you check.

49
00:02:36.759 --> 00:02:37.850
Say it out loud.

50
00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:43.547
So you send the downtime log by Thursday, and I take out the shift comparison.

51
00:02:44.254 --> 00:02:46.193
Shall we look at it together on Monday?

52
00:02:47.237 --> 00:02:50.105
Then confirm it in two lines, the same day.

53
00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:55.030
The typical failure: a good talk, warm feelings, no agreement,

54
00:02:55.251 --> 00:02:57.027
and next week the same problem.

55
00:02:58.061 --> 00:03:02.380
A sharp closing question: what will each of us do differently from tomorrow?

56
00:03:03.686 --> 00:03:04.568
An example.

57
00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.415
Week 3 at a packaging plant.

58
00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.340
Henk, a team leader in his fifties,

59
00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.348
tells his shift in front of you:

60
00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:16.075
this consultant thinks he knows our line after two weeks.

61
00:03:17.086 --> 00:03:21.114
Your analysis is due Friday, and you still need his line data.

62
00:03:21.866 --> 00:03:24.617
You felt your face go red, and said nothing.

63
00:03:25.636 --> 00:03:28.852
Book twenty minutes alone with him, today or tomorrow.

64
00:03:29.866 --> 00:03:30.969
Prepare the layers.

65
00:03:31.684 --> 00:03:34.992
The facts: what he said, and the data you did not get.

66
00:03:35.761 --> 00:03:40.498
His likely story: an outsider judging his line, and his team getting the blame.

67
00:03:41.186 --> 00:03:43.601
Your feelings: embarrassed and irritated.

68
00:03:44.337 --> 00:03:47.774
And your identity trap: wanting to prove you are competent.

69
00:03:48.844 --> 00:03:53.546
Your goal: the data by Wednesday, and Henk checking the analysis before Friday.

70
00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.222
You open like this.

71
00:03:56.967 --> 00:04:00.601
Henk, I want to talk about Monday's remark and about the data,

72
00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:02.970
because I need your help to get this right.

73
00:04:04.081 --> 00:04:06.403
Then: when you said that in front of the shift,

74
00:04:06.542 --> 00:04:09.038
it made it harder for me to get people to talk.

75
00:04:09.806 --> 00:04:10.828
What is behind it?

76
00:04:11.883 --> 00:04:12.707
Then you listen.

77
00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:15.125
And you end with the agreement.
